{
  "an4.38:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 4.38 ",
  "an4.38:0.2": "4. Situations ",
  "an4.38:0.3": "Withdrawn ",
  "an4.38:1.1": "“Mendicants, a mendicant has cast aside idiosyncratic interpretations of the truth, has totally given up searching, has stilled the physical process, and is said to be ‘withdrawn’. ",
  "an4.38:1.2": "And how has a mendicant cast aside idiosyncratic interpretations of the truth? ",
  "an4.38:1.3": "Different ascetics and brahmins have different idiosyncratic interpretations of the truth. For example: ",
  "an4.38:1.4": "the cosmos is eternal, or not eternal, or finite, or infinite; the soul and the body are one and the same, or the soul is one thing, the body another; after death, a realized one still exists, or no longer exists, or both still exists and no longer exists, or neither still exists nor no longer exists. ",
  "an4.38:1.5": "A mendicant has cast out, cast aside, thrown out, discarded, let go of, given up, and relinquished all these. ",
  "an4.38:1.6": "That’s how a mendicant has cast aside idiosyncratic interpretations of the truth. ",
  "an4.38:2.1": "And how has a mendicant totally given up searching? ",
  "an4.38:2.2": "It’s when they’ve given up searching for sensual pleasures, for continued existence, and for a spiritual path. ",
  "an4.38:2.3": "That’s how a mendicant has totally given up searching. ",
  "an4.38:3.1": "And how has a mendicant stilled the physical process? ",
  "an4.38:3.2": "It’s when, with the giving up of pleasure and pain and the di